course: dictionary
lesson: 12116
title: Colonised: taking over a place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Colonised is a verb, so it tells you what someone or something did. It is the past form of colonise, so it describes something that already happened. «Long ago, Britain colonised parts of Australia.» Britain took control of that land and sent its people to live there.
explain: The word can also be about nature. «After the volcano erupted, plants slowly colonised the bare black rock.» No one was in charge here: plants moved in, started living there, and grew. Look at the clues around the word to decide which meaning fits.
explain: In history, colonised often means the land was taken from the people already living there, and this brought great harm to them. Words like «the colonised peoples» name the people who were ruled and lost their land. Always ask who did the taking, and what happened to the people who were there first.
fact: Colonised is the past form of colonise, and it can also be used as an adjective.
fact: In history, «colonised» means one country took control of a land and sent its people to live there, often taking the land from people already living there.
fact: In science, «colonised» means that animals or plants moved into a place and started living and growing there.
fact: «Colonised by» tells you who did the taking or moving in: the land was colonised by settlers.
fact: The US spelling is colonized. Both spellings mean exactly the same thing.
exercise: «Long ago, Britain colonised parts of Australia.» What does colonised mean here? | *Took control of the land and sent its people to live there | Gave the land back to its people | Visited for a short holiday | Painted a map of the land
exercise: «After the volcano erupted, plants slowly colonised the bare black rock.» What does colonised mean here? | *Moved in and started growing there | Broke the rock into small pieces | Ran away from the rock | Covered the rock in bright paint
exercise: Which word is the US spelling of colonised? | colonise | coloured | *colonized | colony
test: «The Romans colonised much of Britain.» In this sentence, colonised means? | Sold the land to a farmer | *Took control of the land and lived there | Built a famous wall | Painted pictures of the hills
test: «Ferns colonised the forest floor after the fire.» What does colonised mean here? | Cleared all the trees away | Swam across a lake | *Moved in and started growing there | Burned down completely
exam: «The land was colonised by settlers who took it from the people already living there.» What does this tell you? | A new school was built on the hill | The land was painted bright colours | The people were given a prize | *People from elsewhere took control of the land, taking it from those already living there
